     ppkkgg__iinnffoo — display information on software packages

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppkkgg__iinnffoo [--AAaaCCccddffIIKKLLMMmmPPqqRRSSssttUUvvzz] [--DD _n_a_m_e[=_v_a_l_u_e]] [--EE _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e]
              [--ee _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e] [--ll _s_t_r] [--QQ _s_u_b_s_t_r_i_n_g] [--rr _p_k_g_s_p_e_c]
              [_p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppkkgg__iinnffoo command is used to dump out information for packages, as
     created by pkg_create(1), which may be still packed up or already in‐
     stalled on the system with the pkg_add(1) command.

     The _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e may be the name of an installed package, the pathname to a
     package distribution file, or a URL to a package available through FTP,
     HTTP, HTTPS, or SCP.  ppkkgg__iinnffoo will try to complete _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e with a ver‐
     sion number while looking through installed packages.  If no _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e is
     specified, ppkkgg__iinnffoo shows the names and one-line comments for all in‐
     stalled packages except internal packages.

     When browsing through uninstalled packages, running ppkkgg__iinnffoo --II **..ttggzz
     will report a summary line for each package, so that it is possible to
     run ppkkgg__iinnffoo ppkkggnnaammee..ttggzz to obtain a longer package description, and
     ppkkgg__aadddd --nn ppkkggnnaammee..ttggzz to check that the installation would proceed
     cleanly, including dependencies.

     The following command-line options are supported:

     --AA      Show the names and one-line comments for all installed packages,
             including internal packages.  This option cannot be combined with
             _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e arguments.

     --aa      Show the names and one-line comments for all installed packages
             except internal packages.  This option cannot be combined with
             _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e arguments.  It is the default when neither options nor
             arguments are given.

     --CC      Show certificate information for signed packages.

     --cc      Show the one-line comment field for each package.

     --DD _n_a_m_e[=_v_a_l_u_e]
             Enforce extra options as given by _n_a_m_e, similarly to pkg_add(1).

             SSIIGGNNEERR           List of trusted signers, separated by commas.
                              Corresponds to list of public keys under
                              _/_e_t_c_/_s_i_g_n_i_f_y we want to trust.  Defaults to any
                              key matching ‘*pkg’ for packages, and any key
                              matching ‘*fw’ for firmware.
             ssnnaapp             Force ‘%c’ and ‘%m’ to expand to ‘snapshots’,
                              even on a release kernel.
             uunnssiiggnneedd         Allow opening unsigned packages without warn‐
                              ings/errors (necessary for ports(7), automati‐
                              cally set by the build infrastructure).

     --dd      Show the long-description field for each package.

     --EE _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Look for the package(s) that contains the given _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e.  As a
             faster alternative, note that there is a package, pkglocatedb,
             that contains a locate(1) database of every file in every pack‐
             age.

     --ee _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e
             This option allows you to test for the presence of another (per‐
             haps prerequisite) package from a script.  If the package identi‐
             fied by _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e is currently installed, return 0, otherwise re‐
             turn 1.  In addition, the names of any package(s) found installed
             are printed to stdout unless turned off using the --qq option.

             The given _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e is actually a package specification, as de‐
             scribed in packages-specs(7).  For example, ppkkgg__iinnffoo --ee
             ''nnaammee-->>==11..33'' will match versions 1.3 and later of the _n_a_m_e pack‐
             age.

     --ee _p_k_g_p_a_t_h
             Another variant of this option that uses a pkgpath instead.  A
             pkgpath is a location within the ports tree, as described in
             pkgpath(7).  For example, ppkkgg__iinnffoo --ee xx1111//kkddee//bbaassee33 will match
             any package that was compiled according to
             _$_{_P_O_R_T_S_D_I_R_}_/_x_1_1_/_k_d_e_/_b_a_s_e_3.

     --ff      Show the packing-list instructions for each package.  See
             pkg_create(1) and package(5) for the various annotations.

     --II      Show the name and one-line comment (“index entry”) for each pack‐
             age.  This is done by default if neither options nor arguments
             are given.

     --KK      Prefix file names with category keyword (e.g., @file, @lib).  Al‐
             ways used together with --LL.

     --LL      Show the files within each package.  This is different from just
             viewing the packing-list, since full pathnames for everything are
             generated.

     --ll _s_t_r  Prefix each information category header (see --qq) shown with _s_t_r.
             This is primarily of use to front-end programs that want to re‐
             quest a lot of different information fields at once for a pack‐
             age, but don't necessarily want the output intermingled in such a
             way that they can't organize it.  This lets you add a special to‐
             ken to the start of each field.

     --MM      Show the install-message file (if any) for each package.

     --mm      Show the names and one-line comments for all packages tagged as
             manually installed, omitting those automatically installed as de‐
             pendencies.  This option cannot be combined with _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e argu‐
             ments.

     --PP      Show the pkgpath(7) for each package.  You can easily build a
             subdirlist with this.

     --QQ _s_u_b_s_t_r_i_n_g
             Show the names of all packages in the first repository of the
             package search path containing the _s_u_b_s_t_r_i_n_g in the stems of
             their package names.  A stem is a package name with all version
             and flavor suffixes removed; see pkg_add(1) for more details on
             stems.  If --aa is also specified, show the names of all matching
             packages in all repositories instead.

     --qq      Be “quiet” in emitting report headers and such, just dump the raw
             info (basically, assume a non-human reading).

     --RR      Show which packages require a given package.

     --rr _p_k_g_s_p_e_c
             Check a list for a given _p_k_g_s_p_e_c.  The following arguments are
             names of packages to verify.  Exit status will be augmented by 2
             if none of the packages do match.

     --SS      Show the update signature for each package.  The ‘update
             signature’ is a unique tag showing the package name, a global
             version number, and the version number of every run time depen‐
             dency and shared library used to build this package.

     --ss      Show an estimate of the total size of each package.

     --tt      Show the names and one-line comments for packages which are not
             required by any other packages.  This option cannot be combined
             with _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e arguments.

     --UU      Show the deinstall-message file (if any) for each package.

     --vv      Turn on verbose output.

     --zz      Fuzzy listing option, often used together with --mm.  Only shows
             stems, flavors and branches information.  To be reused with
             pkg_add(1) --ll to recreate a package installation with different
             versions and no ambiguity.  Note that this intentionally does not
             include firmware, as they are not handled by pkg_add(1).

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     PKG_DBDIR   The standard package database directory, _/_v_a_r_/_d_b_/_p_k_g, can be
                 overridden by specifying an alternative directory in the
                 PKG_DBDIR environment variable.

     PKG_PATH    This can be used to specify a colon-separated list of paths
                 to search for package files.  The current directory is always
                 searched first, even if PKG_PATH is set.  If PKG_PATH is
                 used, the suffix “.tgz” is automatically appended to the
                 _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e, whereas searching in the current directory uses
                 _p_k_g_-_n_a_m_e literally.  Each entry consists of a directory name.
                 URL schemes such as FTP, HTTP, HTTPS, or SCP are also appro‐
                 priate.  The current directory may be indicated implicitly by
                 an empty directory name, or explicitly by a single period
                 (‘./’).  Special sequences ‘%a’, ‘%c’, ‘%m’, ‘%v’ will be ex‐
                 panded.

     PKG_TMPDIR  Temporary area where package information files will be ex‐
                 tracted, instead of _/_t_m_p.

     TRUSTED_PKG_PATH
                 Same semantics as PKG_PATH, but it is searched before
                 PKG_PATH and waives any kind of signature checking.

